WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.690
Last week, a team from your club tested

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:06.510
a paper version of their helper with a

3
00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.870
grandmother at the market.

4
00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.710
She couldn’t read the small buttons,

5
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.030
so she asked to speak instead.

6
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:16.740
That one test changed their design.

7
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.620
Today: test with real people, explain how

8
00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.040
it decides, and pitch in two minutes.

9
00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.570
You don’t need code to test an idea.

10
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:29.920
Draw each screen on paper.

11
00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.500
A friend plays the user, and you play the

12
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.040
AI, answering as your design would.

13
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.570
Watch where they get stuck, and

14
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:39.810
don’t help or explain.

15
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.620
Test with the people who’ll really use it:

16
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.460
students, parents, the school office.

17
00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.830
Write down what confused them,

18
00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.310
change the design, and test again.

19
00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.400
Three short rounds teach you more

20
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.580
than a month of guessing.

21
00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.870
People trust what they can understand.

22
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.000
So explain how your AI

23
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.200
decides, in your users’ words.

24
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:08.220
Not “it runs a ranking algorithm”, but

25
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.740
“it finds the document closest to your

26
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:12.080
question, and shows it”.

27
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.220
And say how sure it is.

28
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.320
So, a pitch is a two-minute story that

29
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.000
helps people trust your idea.

30
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.880
It covers the problem, the users,

31
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.160
the data, how it works, the risks,

32
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:27.860
and why it can be trusted.

33
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.440
Think of a weather forecast.

34
00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.510
“Rain tomorrow” is easy to ignore.

35
00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.070
“Seventy percent chance of rain:

36
00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.070
clouds are building over the hills” is

37
00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:41.070
easier to trust and plan around.

38
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:43.540
Explain your AI that way:

39
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.560
what, why, how sure.

40
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:48.160
Quick check!

41
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.780
During a test, the user gets

42
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.340
stuck on your paper prototype.

43
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:53.830
What should you do?

44
00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.840
Watch, take notes, and change it later.

45
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.510
If you explain during the test,

46
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.690
you hide the problem: real users

47
00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.990
won’t have you there.

48
00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.450
Where they get stuck is

49
00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.170
your most useful data.

50
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.390
Some AI is easy to explain.

51
00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.070
A decision tree is a chain of

52
00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:16.950
questions you can follow to see

53
00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.530
exactly why it decided.

54
00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.790
Big models, like

55
00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.770
chatbots, are much harder.

56
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.480
So show their sources, say they

57
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.440
can be wrong, and test more.

58
00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.410
In the app, build a decision tree from

59
00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:35.510
fourteen school days: the weather, and

60
00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.150
whether the class played football at

61
00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:37.330
break.

62
00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.770
Each question sorts the days.

63
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.480
Then explain any decision in one sentence:

64
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:46.680
sunny and humid, so we stay inside.

65
00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:48.540
Your turn.

66
00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.890
You have two minutes to pitch.

67
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.320
Which of these belongs in a

68
00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.300
pitch people can trust?

69
00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:58.250
The risks, and how you’ll handle

70
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.490
them: honesty about what could

71
00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.110
go wrong builds trust.

72
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.330
Remember: test on paper with real

73
00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:08.250
users, then change it and retest.

74
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.300
Explain how it decides, in

75
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.300
your users’ words.

76
00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.740
And pitch the problem, users, data,

77
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.060
how it works, risks and trust.

78
00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.140
Try it yourself: pitch your

79
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.180
solution in two minutes to your

80
00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.120
class, your family or the AI club.

81
00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.090
Collect three questions people ask you.

82
00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:33.710
Then use them to improve your design.
